{"course": {"schema_version": "1.0", "course_id": "mitocw-2-004-spring-2008", "institution": "Massachusetts Institute of Technology", "platform": "MIT OpenCourseWare", "course_codes": ["2.004"], "cross_listed_codes": [], "course_title": "Dynamics and Control II", "course_slug": "2-004-dynamics-and-control-ii-spring-2008", "term": "Spring", "year": 2008, "as_taught_in": "Spring 2008", "language": "en", "departments": ["Mechanical Engineering"], "academic_levels": ["Undergraduate"], "instructors": ["Prof. Derek Rowell"], "subject_primary": "Mechanical Engineering", "subject_secondary": ["Mathematics", "Applied Mathematics"], "topic_tree": [{"name": "Engineering", "subtopics": [{"name": "Mechanical Engineering", "specialties": []}]}, {"name": "Mathematics", "subtopics": [{"name": "Applied Mathematics", "specialties": []}]}], "topic_keywords": [], "pedagogy_tags": [], "assessment_types": [], "course_feature_tags": ["Lecture Notes", "Problem Set Solutions", "Problem Sets"], "description": "Upon successful completion of this course, students will be able to: Create lumped parameter models (expressed as ODEs) of simple dynamic systems in the electrical and mechanical energy domains Make quantitative estimates of model parameters from experimental measurements Obtain the time-domain response of linear systems to initial conditions and/or common forcing functions (specifically; impulse, step and ramp input) by both analytical and computational methods Obtain the frequency-domain response of linear systems to sinusoidal inputs Compensate the transient response of dynamic systems using feedback techniques Design, implement and test an active control system to achieve a desired performance measure Mastery of these topics will be assessed via homework, quizzes/exams, and lab assignments.", "course_url": "https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/2-004-dynamics-and-control-ii-spring-2008/", "image_url": "https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/2-004-dynamics-and-control-ii-spring-2008/c4768f524c35138e3b5f